    "https://apply.workable.com/university-of-mount-saint-vincent/j/4C82A6C1C5/": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty - Introduction to Ethics",
      "description": "Adjunct Faculty - Introduction to Ethics. 512 - Philosophy and Religious Studies Founded by the Sisters of Charity of New York, the University of Mount Saint Vincent is an academically excellent, authentically inclusive, Catholic and ecumenical liberal arts university. The University combines a strong core curriculum with a full array of majors in the liberal arts and, within the tradition of liberal education, selected professional fields of study. At Mount Saint Vincent, a student’s education extends beyond knowledge, skills, and preparation for work. We seek the development of the whole person. In the spirit of Vincent de Paul and Elizabeth Ann Seton, we foster an understanding of our common humanity, a commitment to human dignity, and a full appreciation of our obligations to each other. Start Date - January 18, 2027 Course schedule - Mondays & Wednesdays 11:15 AM to 12:40 PM 12:50PM to 2:15PM Job Description: The Department of Philosophy invites applications for an instructor to teach Introduction to Ethics , an undergraduate course that introduces students to philosophy through the examination of core ethical questions. Course Description: Introduction to Ethics explores foundational topics in moral philosophy, including: Moral obligation Virtue Justice Law Good and evil Students will engage with primary philosophical texts, develop critical reasoning skills, and reflect on the nature of the good life. A graduate degree (MA or PhD) in Philosophy, or current enrollment in a Philosophy PhD program Demonstrated commitment to teaching and close engagement with students Prior teaching experience in higher education is strongly preferred Allotted salary - $1,100 per credit"
    },
    "https://apply.workable.com/university-of-mount-saint-vincent/j/858609EBB5/": {
      "title": "Graduate Advisor",
      "description": "Graduate Advisor. 517 - Oxley Integrated Advising Program Founded by the Sisters of Charity of New York, the University of Mount Saint Vincent is an academically excellent, authentically inclusive, Catholic and ecumenical liberal arts university. The University combines a strong core curriculum with a full array of majors in the liberal arts and, within the tradition of liberal education, selected professional fields of study. At Mount Saint Vincent, a student’s education extends beyond knowledge, skills, and preparation for work. We seek the development of the whole person. In the spirit of Vincent de Paul and Elizabeth Ann Seton, we foster an understanding of our common humanity, a commitment to human dignity, and a full appreciation of our obligations to each other. Through its School of Professional and Continuing Studies, the University extends its primary undergraduate mission by offering high quality graduate studies in business, education and nursing and an array of undergraduate and certificate programs serving non-traditional students. Job Description: Reporting to the Director of Advising and Student Success and the Dean of the School of Education, this virtual position will work with online graduate education students to develop action plans, provide individual advising, promote student success and retention, and help the student to explore and access academic and career. This exciting opportunity requires an individual who is highly adaptable, attentive to details, has strong organizational skills, works well with a diverse population, and possesses effective communication skills. This is a part-time; 15 hours weekly, 12 month position requiring set day-time availability. Responsibilities: Provide academic advising to graduate online students enrolled in the School of Education at the University of Mount Saint Vincent, including assistance with registration, major selection, withdrawal from courses, leave of absences, withdrawal from the program. Support onboarding and registration of incoming students. Provide comprehensive needs assessment, academic planning, consistent outreach promoting registration, and on-going monitoring of participants progress toward retention and graduation. Encourage persistence and completion by helping online adult learners find ways to overcome both academic and “real-life” barriers and to identify strategies for success. Assist student in interpreting academic policies, program requirements and institutional procedures. Respond to Student Alert forms submitted to the School of Education as well as identifying and conducting prompt outreach to students who are required to retake a course or who do not meet the criteria for good academic standing. Design and deliver student success workshops on topics that focus on student wellness and executive functioning such as stress management, time management, and study skills for students in the designated programs. Make referrals to other points of service such as counseling, financial aid, career services, student life, etc. Design an academic program for “at risk” students and meet more frequently with students that have been placed on academic warning and probation. Guide students in understanding licensure and certification requirements of their program. Support the College mission based on value-oriented education, rooted in the Catholic tradition. Participate in professional development opportunities. Remain current in high impact practices as related to academic advising, student success, and higher education. Other duties as assigned. Requirements; Bachelors' degree, Masters' preferred Knowledge of graduate education, teacher preparation, certification/licensure requirements, and online learning environments. Proficient in Microsoft 365 - Word, Excel, PowerPoint etc., Higher Education experience. Specifically experience using student information systems and retention management tools. Ability to build rapport with diverse student populations- Bilingual in English/Spanish preferred Ability to motivate and encourage adult learners to improve their academic performance using effective goal-setting strategies Creativity and flexibility to personalize their learning approach for individual students Patience and compassion for students as they develop skills and improve their performance Allotted hourly rate - $32.00"
    },
    "https://apply.workable.com/university-of-mount-saint-vincent/j/4226F65C82/": {
      "title": "Seton College | College Persistence and Learning Specialist",
      "description": "Seton College | College Persistence and Learning Specialist. 535 - Seton College Founded by the Sisters of Charity of New York, the University of Mount Saint Vincent is an academically excellent, authentically inclusive, Catholic and ecumenical liberal arts university. The University combines a strong core curriculum with a full array of majors in the liberal arts and, within the tradition of liberal education, selected professional fields of study. At Mount Saint Vincent, a student’s education extends beyond knowledge, skills, and preparation for work. We seek the development of the whole person. In the spirit of Vincent de Paul and Elizabeth Ann Seton, we foster an understanding of our common humanity, a commitment to human dignity, and a full appreciation of our obligations to each other. About Seton College Seton College is a new two-year college division of UMSV that offers an associate’s degree for students that have been historically underserved, who may require additional academic support, and who are motivated for post-secondary success. The program’s goal is to prepare students to enroll in a bachelor’s degree program. Seton College students will study on the Riverdale campus of UMSV and have full access to UMSV’s facilities and programs. Applicants may learn more about Seton College by clicking HERE Position Summary Seton College invites applications for a College Persistence and Learning Specialist to strengthen academic readiness, persistence, and degree completion. The College Persistence and Learning Specialist will support and provide direct, high touch learning support to current Seton students. Working in close partnership with faculty, academic leadership, advising, and student-support staff, the Specialist will focus on the “how” of college success: helping students develop the learning strategies, executive-functioning skills, academic habits, and self-efficacy needed to succeed in rigorous college-level coursework. The position will also work closely with faculty to develop scalable resources, support professional development, and contribute to a culture of academic rigor and support. Primary Responsibilities Teaching and Learning Develop scalable tools, learning resources, and programming that strengthen a college-wide culture of academic rigor, engagement, persistence, and support. Collaborate with faculty and academic leadership to identify patterns in student learning needs and develop interventions. Partner with the Associate Dean to support college-wide assessment and intervention efforts. Lead faculty workshops and professional development. Maintain appropriate records of student learning outcomes and contribute to assessment of academic support initiatives, including retention, credit attainment, GPA, academic standing, persistence, and degree progression. Academic Intervention Provide individualized and small-group academic coaching focused on executive functioning, time management, goal setting, study strategies, note-taking, critical reading, test preparation, and self regulated learning. Identify and support students experiencing academic difficulty through proactive outreach, early intervention, individualized learning plans, and structured progress monitoring. Facilitate student study groups and structured learning sessions. Oversee and develop Supplemental Instruction (SI), including recruitment, training, supervision, and support of peer leaders and alignment of SI with course and faculty needs. Partner with faculty, advisors, and student-support staff to coordinate timely academic interventions for students at risk of Academic Warning, Recovery, Suspension, or other barriers to satisfactory academic progress. Other Duties Attend faculty and staff meetings and participate in College-wide student success initiatives. Perform other duties related to student learning, persistence, and academic success as assigned. Some evening and weekend responsibilities may be required. Master’s degree required in education, psychology, teaching and learning, reading/literacy, higher education, counseling, or a closely related field; EdD, PhD, or other terminal degree preferred. At least five years of relevant experience in teaching, academic intervention, student success, or a related area. Systems-level thinker with demonstrated knowledge of learning strategies, executive functioning, academic skill development, and evidence-informed approaches to student persistence. Demonstrated ability to work effectively with first-generation college students from diverse cultural and educational backgrounds. Experience providing individualized academic coaching or intervention and facilitating workshops, study groups, or other group-based learning supports. Strong collaboration and communication skills, including the ability to partner effectively with faculty, advisors, and student-support professionals. Experience with academic interventions and multi-tiered student-support models is strongly preferred. Commitment to undergraduate learning and to an asset-based approach that recognizes students’ strengths while providing the structure and strategies needed for academic success The salary range is $85,000 - $90,000 Comprehensive health, dental, and vision insurance Flexible Spending Accounts (FSA) for medical and dependent care 100% employer sponsored life insurance, AD&D insurance and Long term disability Retirement plan with employer matching Paid time off, including vacation, sick leave, and holidays Professional development opportunities via Coursera Tuition benefits for their dependents Commuter benefits Employee Assistance Plan"
    },
    "https://apply.workable.com/university-of-mount-saint-vincent/j/9BEB487AFB/": {
      "title": "Admissions Operations Assistant",
      "description": "Admissions Operations Assistant. 803 - Admissions Founded by the Sisters of Charity of New York, the University of Mount Saint Vincent is an academically excellent, authentically inclusive, Catholic and ecumenical liberal arts university. The University combines a strong core curriculum with a full array of majors in the liberal arts and, within the tradition of liberal education, selected professional fields of study. At Mount Saint Vincent, a student’s education extends beyond knowledge, skills, and preparation for work. We seek the development of the whole person. In the spirit of Vincent de Paul and Elizabeth Ann Seton, we foster an understanding of our common humanity, a commitment to human dignity, and a full appreciation of our obligations to each other. Through its School of Professional and Continuing Studies, the University extends its primary undergraduate mission by offering high quality graduate studies in business, education and nursing and an array of undergraduate and certificate programs serving non-traditional students. Job Description: The Admissions Operations Assistant provides administrative and operational support to the Enrollment Division and plays an important role in ensuring the efficient day-to-day operations of the Admissions Department. Responsibilities include maintaining accurate student records, processing admissions materials, supporting office mailings, and completing routine data entry and document management tasks. The position follows established procedures and works under the direction of Admissions leadership to provide consistent and reliable operational support. While the position may work with systems such as Slate and Banner, it is not responsible for the overall administration, configuration, or strategic management of these systems. Success in this role requires reliability, accuracy, discretion, strong attention to detail, and a willingness to support both technology-based initiatives and hands-on office operations. Job Responsibilities: Provide general administrative and operational support UMSV’s Graduate and Undergraduate Admissions teams. Process, organize, scan, file, and appropriately dispose of admissions documents and other office materials, including confidential document shredding. Assist with incoming and outgoing email, physical mail, admissions mailings, acceptance materials, event materials, and other large-scale communication projects. Sort, review, clean, and organize Excel spreadsheets and other data files. Perform routine data entry and record maintenance in Slate, Banner, and other university systems. Review student records for completeness and assist with identifying missing or incorrectly entered information. Upload, match, and organize application materials and supporting documents within student records. Assist with routine Slate operational tasks, queries, exports, and data cleanup as assigned. Support data reconciliation between Slate, Banner, spreadsheets, and other institutional records. Conduct secondary reviews of admission applications and supporting materials when trained and assigned. Assist with processing transcripts and other admissions credentials in accordance with established office procedures including their evaluation and upload Prepare materials for recruitment events, campus visits, admitted student programs, and other Admissions initiatives. Assemble packets, labels, envelopes, giveaway materials, and other supplies for Admissions programs and communications Support audits and data-quality projects to ensure admissions records are accurate and complete. Perform other administrative and operational duties as assigned to support the day-to-day functions of Admissions. Associate degree or higher required. Candidates with a high school diploma with equivalent and relevant experience in CRM systems, database management, or related administrative operations will also be considered. Strong organizational skills and attention to detail. Ability to accurately complete administrative and data-entry tasks. Basic proficiency with Microsoft Office, particularly Excel, Outlook, and Word. Comfort learning and working within database and technology systems, spreadsheets, and able to manage repetitive administrative tasks with a high degree of accuracy. Ability to handle confidential student information with discretion and in accordance with university policies. Previous experience with Slate, Banner, or other higher education student information or CRM systems is preferred but not required. The allotted hourly rate is $26.50."
    },
    "https://apply.workable.com/university-of-mount-saint-vincent/j/E057962332/": {
      "title": "Seton College | Student Success and Engagement Coordinator",
      "description": "Seton College | Student Success and Engagement Coordinator. 535 - Seton College Founded by the Sisters of Charity of New York, the University of Mount Saint Vincent is an academically excellent, authentically inclusive, Catholic and ecumenical liberal arts university. The University combines a strong core curriculum with a full array of majors in the liberal arts and, within the tradition of liberal education, selected professional fields of study. At Mount Saint Vincent, a student’s education extends beyond knowledge, skills, and preparation for work. We seek the development of the whole person. In the spirit of Vincent de Paul and Elizabeth Ann Seton, we foster an understanding of our common humanity, a commitment to human dignity, and a full appreciation of our obligations to each other. About Seton College Seton College is a new two-year college division of UMSV that offers an associate’s degree for students that have been historically underserved, who may require additional academic support, and who are motivated for post-secondary success. The program’s goal is to prepare students to enroll in a bachelor’s degree program. Seton College students will study on the Riverdale campus of UMSV and have full access to UMSV’s facilities and programs. Applicants may learn more about Seton College by clicking HERE Overview The Student Success and Engagement Coordinator supports the holistic development, well-being, and engagement of students and graduates at Seton College. This role is essential to Seton’s high-touch academic support model and helps students successfully transition into and navigate college life by connecting them with campus resources, student engagement opportunities, and wellness support. This role also supports graduates as they transition and enroll in bachelor's degree programs. Working collaboratively with campus life staff, faculty, and community partners, the role helps foster a welcoming and inclusive environment where students can build meaningful connections, access support, and actively participate in the campus community. By getting to know students and through outreach, programming, and resource coordination, the position promotes student success, personal growth, and a strong sense of belonging. Duties and Responsibilities Provides high-touch, holistic support and guidance to students enrolled in Seton College, promoting their academic success, personal development, and engagement in campus life. Proactively builds appropriate relationships with students; serves as a key point of contact to help students acclimate to the college environment by connecting them with campus resources, student organizations, leadership opportunities, and co-curricular programming. Works collaboratively with Seton College and UMSV staff and faculty, and advisors to support student development and foster a sense of belonging within the campus community. Assists students in navigating college systems and resources, including but not limited to academic support services, financial aid, wellness resources, and student engagement. Provides guidance and support to students to help facilitate student success and adjustment to college life. In collaboration with Seton College staff and campus partners, develops and implements programming that promotes student well-being, personal growth, leadership development, and community engagement. Supports the planning and delivery of major programming initiatives within Seton College, including summer program, summer orientation, wellness and student development, leadership and college engagement, career exploration opportunities, community-building events and others. Maintains strong knowledge of internal campus services as well as external community and government resources, and facilitates referrals when appropriate. Serves as a liaison between Seton College, campus life offices, and community organizations to enhance student access to support services and engagement opportunities. Responds to student concerns and challenges in collaboration with faculty and staff, helping students access available resources and supports. Participates in training, campus initiatives, and collaborative planning efforts related to student success, engagement, and wellness. Acts as a resource to staff and faculty regarding student wellness, engagement, and factors that may impact student success. Supports and sponsors student leadership and engagement initiatives, including serving as an advisor to student council and other clubs/programs as directed by the Associate Dean. Work closely with the Associate Deans and student-support staff to ensure successful transition and persistence of Seton graduates to bachelor’s degree programs. Serve as a dedicated college persistence resource for Seton graduates enrolled in bachelor’s degree programs, providing proactive outreach, goal-setting support, assistance navigating institutional processes, and connection to campus and community resources. Maintains appropriate records and documentation and prepares written reports and communications as needed. Performs other duties as assigned. Bachelor’s degree required; 3-5 years of relevant work experience, or a combination of education and experience from which comparable knowledge and skills are acquired. Demonstrated experience and expertise in student engagement, education, advocacy, counseling, advising, coaching, or other student-centered work. A history of success with project management and program development. Strong writing, communication, leadership, and interpersonal skills. Ability to work independently and as a part of a team. Able to develop rapport and build trust with a diverse range of individuals. Strong record of using data to inform program design and improvement. Requires strong skills in advising, coordinating, group learning, public speaking, and teaching. Knowledgeable of opportunity programs such as Higher Education Opportunity Program, TRiO and other college access programs. Experience in crisis intervention preferred. Experience building relationships with external/community partners preferred. Verbal and written communications skills in English and Spanish preferred. The salary range is $66,350 - $68,000 Comprehensive health, dental, and vision insurance Flexible Spending Accounts (FSA) for medical and dependent care 100% employer sponsored life insurance, AD&D insurance and Long term disability Retirement plan with employer matching Paid time off, including vacation, sick leave, and holidays Professional development opportunities via Coursera Tuition benefits for their dependents Commuter benefits Employee Assistance Plan"
    },
    "https://apply.workable.com/university-of-mount-saint-vincent/j/FC9015B4CF/": {
      "title": "Associate Director for Admission",
      "description": "Associate Director for Admission. 803 - Admissions Founded by the Sisters of Charity of New York, the University of Mount Saint Vincent is an academically excellent, authentically inclusive, Catholic and ecumenical liberal arts university. The University combines a strong core curriculum with a full array of majors in the liberal arts and, within the tradition of liberal education, selected professional fields of study. At Mount Saint Vincent, a student’s education extends beyond knowledge, skills, and preparation for work. We seek the development of the whole person. In the spirit of Vincent de Paul and Elizabeth Ann Seton, we foster an understanding of our common humanity, a commitment to human dignity, and a full appreciation of our obligations to each other. Through its School of Professional and Continuing Studies, the University extends its primary undergraduate mission by offering high quality graduate studies in business, education and nursing and an array of undergraduate and certificate programs serving non-traditional students. Job Description: The Associate Director for Admission is responsible, identifying, recruiting and enrolling incoming prospective high school students and other populations as required while providing guidance to Assistant and Senior Assistant Directors of Admissions. This roll also provides support to the Director and Enrollment Division. Responsibilities: Leadership & Supervision Develop and maintain strong understanding and knowledge of the University, its academic and student life programs, and policies in order to communicate accurate and current information to prospective students. Provide guidance, mentorship, and performance feedback to members of the admission team. Serves as Director and primary point of contact in the absence of the Director Working with Director of Undergraduate Admission, lead training for new counselors and support professional development for the team. Manage at least one strategic initiative or functional area (e.g., Slate CRM workflows, transfer recruitment, counselor engagements, data reporting, direct admission, campus events, or digital marketing pipelines). Assist in setting annual enrollment targets, project timelines, and recruitment strategies. Serve as a point of contact for designated academic programs, schools, or student pipelines. Recruitment & Outreach Manage a defined recruitment territory with clear goals and travel expectations. Represent UMSV at high schools, college fairs, specialized programs, and virtual events. Develop and sustain relationships with school counselors, CBOs, and community partners. Support new and emerging recruitment strategies—including direct admission, College Vine, virtual student engagement, etc. Application Review & Yield: Review applications for admission and make informed recommendations. Maintain constant communication via phone, email, text and chat with individual prospective students throughout the recruitment funnel; participate in follow up activities as defined by the Director. Monitor student progression through the funnel; use Slate to drive activity and follow-ups. Support financial aid conversations and assist students & families navigate the enrollment process. Lead or contribute to yield and anti-melt campaigns, including text/call/email follow-ups. Event & Project Coordination: Provide leadership support for major events such as Open House, Accepted Student Days, counselor receptions, and other campus visits. Coordinate or oversee communication campaigns to prospective/funnel students. Assist with staff scheduling, travel planning, recruitment calendar, and visit logistics. Data, Reporting & Strategy: Track recruitment progress using Slate and enrollment dashboards. Analyze outcomes to inform decision-making and strategy adjustments. Provide weekly reports and enrollment updates to the Director of Admission. Contribute to annual planning, goal setting, and territory performance reports. Collaboration & Institutional Partnership: Work closely with Financial Aid, Athletics, Advisement & Retention, and Academic Affairs. Represent Admissions on cross-functional committees when appropriate. Support alignment of messaging across academic programs, marketing, and admissions materials. Participate in University-wide events. Other duties as assigned. Bachelor's degree Valid drivers license and the ability to travel Availability to work evening and weekend events. Strong interpersonal and organizational skills, oral and written communication skills, attention to detail and follow up protocols required. Knowledge of Slate CRM, Microsoft Office, Excel required, familiarity with admission systems Banner and Argos desirable. Ability to work effectively with all University personnel, students, and the community. Candidates should have an interest in higher education, a work environment that demands flexibility, encourages creativity and collegiality, while emphasizing accountability for meeting goals. The allotted annual salary is $75,000 Comprehensive health, dental, and vision insurance Flexible Spending Accounts (FSA) for medical and dependent care 100% employer sponsored life insurance, AD&D insurance and Long term disability Retirement plan with employer matching Paid time off, including vacation, sick leave, and holidays Professional development opportunities via Coursera Tuition benefits for their dependents Commuter benefits Employee Assistance Plan"
    },
    "https://apply.workable.com/university-of-mount-saint-vincent/j/B51AEB0384/": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Assistant Professor of Psychology- Social Psychology",
      "description": "Tenure-Track Assistant Professor of Psychology- Social Psychology. 513 - Psychology Founded by the Sisters of Charity of New York, the University of Mount Saint Vincent is an academically excellent, authentically inclusive, Catholic and ecumenical liberal arts university. The University combines a strong core curriculum with a full array of majors in the liberal arts and, within the tradition of liberal education, selected professional fields of study. At Mount Saint Vincent, a student’s education extends beyond knowledge, skills, and preparation for work. We seek the development of the whole person. In the spirit of Vincent de Paul and Elizabeth Ann Seton, we foster an understanding of our common humanity, a commitment to human dignity, and a full appreciation of our obligations to each other. Open until filled. Applications submitted by December 15, 2026 will be given priority. Start Date: August 2027 Job Description: The Psychology Department at the University of Mount Saint Vincent invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track position at the rank of Assistant Professor, beginning August 2027. We seek a social psychologist whose teaching and research will complement and strengthen the department's existing programs. The successful candidate will demonstrate a strong commitment to excellence in undergraduate teaching and to supporting a diverse student population within an urban liberal arts college. The teaching load is 21 credits per academic year and will include coursework in introductory psychology, social psychology and other courses related to the candidate’s areas of expertise, and eventually the department’s senior capstone course. The successful candidate will also have opportunities to develop new courses that contribute to the department’s evolving curriculum. The Psychology Department values faculty mentorship and the active involvement of undergraduates in research. Candidates should demonstrate the potential to maintain an active program of scholarship that is appropriate to the University’s teaching-focused mission and that creates meaningful opportunities for undergraduate participation. Service responsibilities include academic advising for Psychology majors, participation in departmental programs and activities, and service on University governance committees. Job Responsibilities: Teaching Load : 21 credits per year, including: Introductory psychology and Social Psychology courses Courses in the candidate’s area of specialization Other foundational courses in the psychology major Opportunity to develop new courses for the curriculum Research : Engage undergraduates in an active research program within the department Research support available through various University funding sources Demonstrate the potential to maintain an active program of scholarship appropriate to the University’s teaching-focused mission and provide meaningful opportunities for undergraduate research participation Service Responsibilities: Academic advising for students Participation in departmental and University activities Serving on University governance committees Doctorate in Social Psychology or a closely related field from an accredited institution; candidates who are ABD will be considered Training and scholarly expertise in social psychology Evidence of, or demonstrated potential for, excellence in undergraduate teaching Commitment to teaching and mentoring students from diverse backgrounds Potential to maintain an active research program that involves undergraduate students Commitment to departmental service and University governance Candidates must be legally authorized to work in the United States at the time of appointment. The University is unable to provide employment-visa sponsorship for this position. Applicants should submit the following application materials A cover letter describing their qualifications, area of specialization, and interest in the position Curriculum vitae Statement of teaching philosophy Evidence of teaching effectiveness Research statement, including plans for involving undergraduate students Three letters of recommendation The allotted salary range for this position is $68,000 to $70,000 Medical, dental, vision insurance Flexible Spending Account (FSA) Enhanced Short Term Disability Insurance 100% employer sponsored Long Term Disability Voluntary Life Insurance Commuter Benefits Contribution in a 403(b) Retirement Plan Employee Assistance Program"
    },
    "https://apply.workable.com/university-of-mount-saint-vincent/j/AC6E556143/": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Assistant Professor of Psychology-Open Area",
      "description": "Tenure-Track Assistant Professor of Psychology-Open Area. 513 - Psychology Founded by the Sisters of Charity of New York, the University of Mount Saint Vincent is an academically excellent, authentically inclusive, Catholic and ecumenical liberal arts university. The University combines a strong core curriculum with a full array of majors in the liberal arts and, within the tradition of liberal education, selected professional fields of study. At Mount Saint Vincent, a student’s education extends beyond knowledge, skills, and preparation for work. We seek the development of the whole person. In the spirit of Vincent de Paul and Elizabeth Ann Seton, we foster an understanding of our common humanity, a commitment to human dignity, and a full appreciation of our obligations to each other. Open until filled. Applications submitted by December 15, 2026 will be given priority. Start date: August 2027 Job Description: The Psychology Department at the University of Mount Saint Vincent invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track position at the rank of Assistant Professor, beginning August 2027. Although the area of specialization is open, the department is particularly interested in candidates whose teaching and research expertise falls within one of the following areas: •Forensic psychology or psychology and law •Neurodevelopment/neurodiversity within a neuropsychology, biological psychology, health psychology, school psychology, or educational psychology framework The successful candidate will demonstrate a strong commitment to excellence in undergraduate teaching and to supporting a diverse student population within an urban liberal arts college. The teaching load is 21 credits per academic year and will include introductory psychology, courses related to the candidate’s area of expertise, and eventually the department’s senior capstone course. The successful candidate will also have opportunities to develop new courses that expand and strengthen the department’s curriculum. The Psychology Department values faculty mentorship and the active involvement of undergraduates in research. Candidates should demonstrate the potential to maintain an active program of scholarship appropriate to the University’s teaching-focused mission and to provide meaningful opportunities for undergraduate research participation. Service responsibilities include advising Psychology majors, participating in departmental programs and activities, and serving on University governance committees. Job Responsibilities: Teaching Load : 21 credits per year, including: Introductory psychology courses Courses in the candidate’s area of specialization Other foundational courses in the psychology major Opportunity to develop new courses for the curriculum Research : Engage undergraduates in an active research program within the department Research support available through various University funding sources Demonstrate the potential to maintain an active program of scholarship appropriate to the University’s teaching-focused mission and provide meaningful opportunities for undergraduate research participation Service Responsibilities: Academic advising for students Participation in departmental and University activities Serving on University governance committees Doctorate in Psychology or a closely related field from an accredited institution; candidates who are ABD on track to confer their degree by August 2027 will be considered Teaching or research expertise in one of the department’s priority areas (e.g., biological, cognitive, social, sociocultural, developmental, clinical) Evidence of, or demonstrated potential for, excellence in undergraduate teaching Commitment to teaching and mentoring students from diverse backgrounds Potential to maintain an active research program involving undergraduate students Commitment to departmental service and University governance Legal authorization to work in the United States at the time of appointment (the University is unable to provide employment-visa sponsorship for this position) Preferred Qualifications Teaching or research expertise in forensic psychology or psychology and law; or Teaching or research expertise involving neurodivergent populations within neuropsychology, biological psychology, health psychology, school psychology, or educational psychology frameworks. Relevant areas of scholarship may include autism, ADHD, learning disabilities, intellectual disabilities, cognitive development, academic functioning, psychosocial adjustment, or health and quality-of-life outcomes. Experience mentoring undergraduate students in research Ability to develop courses that complement and expand the department’s existing curriculum Applicants should submit the following materials A cover letter describing their qualifications, area of specialization, and interest in the position Curriculum vitae Statement of teaching philosophy Evidence of teaching effectiveness Research statement, including plans for involving undergraduate students Three letters of recommendation Salary Range: $68,000 - $70,000 per year, commensurate with experience and qualifications. Comprehensive health, dental, and vision insurance Flexible Spending Accounts (FSA) for medical and dependent care 100% employer sponsored life insurance, AD&D insurance and Long term disability Retirement plan with employer matching Paid time off, including vacation, sick leave, and holidays Professional development opportunities via Coursera Tuition benefits for their dependents Commuter benefits Employee Assistance Plan"
    },
    "https://apply.workable.com/university-of-mount-saint-vincent/j/3E4C1AC19D/": {
      "title": "Executive Assistant to the President",
      "description": "Executive Assistant to the President. 101 - Office of the President Founded by the Sisters of Charity of New York, the University of Mount Saint Vincent is an academically excellent, authentically inclusive, Catholic and ecumenical liberal arts university. The University combines a strong core curriculum with a full array of majors in the liberal arts and, within the tradition of liberal education, selected professional fields of study. At Mount Saint Vincent, a student’s education extends beyond knowledge, skills, and preparation for work. We seek the development of the whole person. In the spirit of Vincent de Paul and Elizabeth Ann Seton, we foster an understanding of our common humanity, a commitment to human dignity, and a full appreciation of our obligations to each other. Position Summary: The Executive Assistant to the President provides high-level administrative and operational support to the President and ensures the smooth day-to-day functioning of the Office of the President. This highly visible and confidential role requires exceptional organization, sound judgment, discretion, responsiveness, and the ability to manage multiple priorities in a fast-paced environment. The Executive Assistant also provides basic administrative support to the Vice President for Strategy, primarily related to scheduling, meeting coordination, travel logistics, and other routine administrative needs. The President establishes final prioritization when competing demands arise. This is a full-time, exempt, on-site position. Occasional evening or weekend availability may be required for presidential events, institutional activities, and other mission-critical needs. Review of applications will begin immediately. For priority consideration, applicants should submit all application materials by October 15, 2026 . The position will remain open until filled, and applications received after the priority date may be considered as needed. Responsibilities: Manage the President’s calendar, including scheduling, prioritizing appointments, coordinating meetings, and protecting time for the President’s highest priorities. Provide calendar and scheduling support for the Vice President for Strategy and coordinate routine administrative logistics as needed, Coordinate domestic and international travel arrangements for the President and, as assigned, the Vice President for Strategy, working with other University offices when appropriate. As assigned, prepare, format, and proofread correspondence, meeting materials, reports, presentations, agendas, and other documents with exceptional accuracy and attention to detail. Ensure the President has appropriate agendas, background materials, schedules, and logistical information in advance of significant meetings and engagements Anticipate the President’s administrative needs and ensure that materials, schedules, logistics, and follow-up items are organized in advance of meetings and events. Manage confidential documents and sensitive information with the highest level of discretion and professionalism. Coordinate logistics for presidential meetings, events, visitors, and engagements, including room reservations, catering, technology, and related arrangements. Screen and manage incoming calls, emails, requests, and inquiries to the Office of the President, directing matters appropriately and ensuring timely follow-up. Maintain a professional, welcoming, and service-oriented Office of the President for students, faculty, staff, trustees, alumni, donors, community partners, and other visitors. Maintain office systems, records, contact lists, files, supplies, and other administrative resources necessary for the effective operation of the President’s Office. Support the President’s Office budget and administrative processes, including purchase requisitions, invoices, reimbursements, and expense documentation. Track routine commitments, deadlines, and follow-up items for the President and help ensure that agreed-upon actions are completed on schedule. Use sound judgment to determine what requires the President’s immediate attention and what can be routed or resolved through other University offices. Use Microsoft Office, calendaring and collaboration platforms, and emerging AI-enabled productivity tools appropriately to improve efficiency and accuracy. Perform other duties as assigned by the President. Sample Performance Measures: The President's & VP for Strategy's calendars are accurate, organized, and include protection of priority time. Meeting and travel materials are prepared accurately and on time. Presidential commitments and follow-up items are reliably tracked and communicated. Travel, meetings, visitors, and events are executed smoothly and at a high standard. The President's Office is responsive, professional, discreet, and well organized. Bachelor’s degree required; equivalent combination of education and relevant experience may be considered. At least five years of progressively responsible administrative or executive support experience; experience supporting a president, CEO, senior executive, or similarly complex office strongly preferred. Exceptional organizational skills and demonstrated ability to manage multiple priorities, competing deadlines, and frequent schedule changes. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, including strong proofreading and document-preparation skills. Demonstrated judgment, discretion, diplomacy, and ability to handle sensitive or confidential matters. Strong interpersonal skills and the ability to work effectively with senior leaders, trustees, faculty, staff, students, alumni, donors, and external partners. High level of initiative, follow-through, reliability, and attention to detail. Ability to work independently while remaining collaborative, responsive, and service-oriented. Advanced proficiency with Microsoft Office and digital productivity tools; comfort learning and appropriately using AI-enabled tools to support executive work. Commitment to the mission, values, and Catholic, Vincentian, and Setonian identity of the University of Mount Saint Vincent. Preferred Qualifications: Experience in higher education, nonprofit leadership, or another complex mission-driven organization. Experience supporting more than one senior executive or managing shared executive-office priorities. Experience coordinating executive travel, events, and high-level meetings. Familiarity with project tracking, contact management, and executive-office workflow systems. The annual salary range for this position is $90,000 to $95,000 commensurate with experience and qualifications. The University offers a comprehensive benefits package designed to support employees’ health, financial well-being, and work-life balance. Benefits for eligible employees include: Paid time off, including vacation and sick leave Medical, dental, and vision insurance Flexible Spending Account (FSA) Enhanced Short-Term Disability coverage Employer-paid Long-Term Disability coverage Voluntary Life Insurance Commuter benefits 403(b) Retirement Plan Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Professional development opportunities Tuition benefits for employees and their dependents Eligibility for benefits is subject to the terms and conditions of the applicable benefit plans and University policies."
    },
