    "https://apply.workable.com/university-of-mount-saint-vincent/j/BC6B28A9C3/": {
      "title": "Director of Data Management",
      "description": "Director of Data Management. 511 - St. Joseph's School of Nursing Founded by the Sisters of Charity of New York, the University of Mount Saint Vincent is an academically excellent, authentically inclusive, Catholic and ecumenical liberal arts university. The University combines a strong core curriculum with a full array of majors in the liberal arts and, within the tradition of liberal education, selected professional fields of study. At Mount Saint Vincent, a student’s education extends beyond knowledge, skills, and preparation for work. We seek the development of the whole person. In the spirit of Vincent de Paul and Elizabeth Ann Seton, we foster an understanding of our common humanity, a commitment to human dignity, and a full appreciation of our obligations to each other. Position Summary: The Director of Data Management serves as the School of Nursing's lead administrator for data analytics, reporting, compliance, and institutional effectiveness. Working closely with the Dean and academic leadership, this position oversees the collection, management, analysis, and reporting of data related to student success, faculty productivity, program outcomes, accreditation, and regulatory compliance. The Director transforms complex data into actionable insights that support strategic planning, continuous quality improvement, accreditation activities, and evidence-based decision-making across the School of Nursing. Responsibilities: Accreditation, Compliance, and Regulatory Reporting Manage data collection and reporting processes required for accreditation, state, and federal regulatory agencies. Prepare and submit reports for organizations including the New York State Education Department (NYSED), Commission on Collegiate Nursing Education (CCNE), and other regulatory bodies. Monitor program outcomes, including retention, graduation, licensure and certification pass rates, employment outcomes, and student learning outcomes. Ensure accuracy, integrity, and compliance of institutional data in accordance with FERPA, HIPAA, and university policies. Maintain and validate data reported on School of Nursing websites, publications, and external reports. Data Analytics and Strategic Decision Support Develop dashboards, reports, and performance metrics that support academic and operational decision-making. Analyze trends related to enrollment, retention, progression, graduation, licensure outcomes, faculty workload, and resource utilization. Provide data analyses and reports to support strategic planning, budgeting, program development, and growth initiatives. Collaborate with leadership to identify opportunities for continuous improvement and operational effectiveness. Data Systems Management Oversee the integration and management of institutional and program-specific data systems. Coordinate data collection, validation, and reporting across academic and administrative departments. Develop efficient processes for data entry, retrieval, analysis, and reporting. Maintain data dictionaries, reporting procedures, and documentation standards. Evaluate and implement technologies that enhance reporting and analytics capabilities. Training and Collaboration Serve as a resource to faculty and staff on data reporting requirements, dashboards, and data management practices. Provide training and support related to data systems and reporting tools. Collaborate with university offices including Institutional Research, Information Technology, Admissions, Registrar, Finance, and Academic Affairs to ensure data quality and consistency. Master's degree in Data Analytics, Statistics, Information Systems, Higher Education Administration, Public Health, Business Analytics, or a related field. Minimum of three years of progressively responsible experience in data management, institutional research, business intelligence, or analytics. Demonstrated experience managing complex datasets and producing reports for leadership and external stakeholders. Proficiency with reporting and data visualization tools such as Power BI, Tableau, or similar platforms. Strong analytical, organizational, communication, and problem-solving skills. Ability to maintain confidentiality and manage sensitive information. Preferred Qualifications: Doctoral degree in a related field. Experience in higher education, healthcare, or nursing education. Knowledge of accreditation standards, outcomes assessment, and regulatory reporting requirements. Experience supporting accreditation reviews and continuous quality improvement initiatives. Salary Range: $85,000 - $90,000 per year, commensurate with experience and qualifications. Comprehensive health, dental, and vision insurance Flexible Spending Accounts (FSA) for medical and dependent care 100% employer sponsored life insurance, AD&D insurance and Long term disability Retirement plan with employer matching Paid time off, including vacation, sick leave, and holidays Professional development opportunities via Coursera Tuition benefits for their dependents Commuter benefits Employee Assistance Plan"
    },
    "https://apply.workable.com/university-of-mount-saint-vincent/j/A3AC8DB9A5/": {
      "title": "Director for Campus Safety",
      "description": "Director for Campus Safety. 407 - Campus Safety and Security Founded by the Sisters of Charity of New York, the University of Mount Saint Vincent is an academically excellent, authentically inclusive, Catholic and ecumenical liberal arts university. The University combines a strong core curriculum with a full array of majors in the liberal arts and, within the tradition of liberal education, selected professional fields of study. At Mount Saint Vincent, a student’s education extends beyond knowledge, skills, and preparation for work. We seek the development of the whole person. In the spirit of Vincent de Paul and Elizabeth Ann Seton, we foster an understanding of our common humanity, a commitment to human dignity, and a full appreciation of our obligations to each other. The Men’s and Women’s Coach is responsible for providing leadership, organization and administration of the Men’s and Women’s program Through its School of Professional and Continuing Studies, the University extends its primary undergraduate mission by offering high quality graduate studies in business, education and nursing and an array of undergraduate and certificate programs serving non-traditional students. Job Description: The Director for Campus Safety is responsible for the overall leadership, management, and operation of the University’s Campus Safety Department. Campus Safety is within the Division of Operations, and the position reports to the VP and AVP for Operations. The Director is responsible for maintaining a safe, secure, professional, and service-oriented environment for students, faculty, staff, Sisters of Charity of New York (SCNY), visitors, and University assets and facilities. This position provides strategic leadership and oversight of the University’s 24/7 Campus Safety operation, including departmental staffing and supervision, patrol and response operations, campus access and visitor management, parking enforcement, security technology, emergency preparedness, incident management, and related safety programs. The Director develops, implements, and maintains policies, procedures, and practices that promote a safe and secure campus environment, support the University’s mission, and ensure compliance with applicable laws, regulations, and industry standards. As an essential employee, this position must remain or report for work during partial or full-day closings of the University due to hazardous or emergency conditions, including inclement weather, as needed. The Director must also be available to respond to significant incidents and emergencies outside of normal business hours. Responsibilities: Department Leadership and Operations Lead and manage the Campus Safety Department, including hiring, training, scheduling, supervision, development, and performance evaluation of Campus Safety personnel. Ensure appropriate 24/7 staffing, supervision, post coverage, patrol operations, and departmental readiness, addressing staffing or operational issues as necessary. Develop, implement, review, and enforce Campus Safety policies, procedures, post orders, emergency response protocols, parking policies, and other departmental standards, and ensure departmental personnel adhere to established University and Campus Safety policies and procedures. Promote a professional, service-oriented, and community-centered approach to Campus Safety and foster a culture of accountability, preparedness, and safety awareness throughout the University. Incident Response and Emergency Preparedness Oversee Campus Safety response to incidents and emergencies and serve as a key member of the University’s emergency response structure. Lead Campus Safety operations during emergencies and coordinate response activities with University leadership and appropriate internal and external partners. Review significant incidents and departmental reports to ensure appropriate response, documentation, notification, follow-up, and referral. Coordinate with law enforcement, fire, EMS, and other agencies when incidents require outside assistance or exceed the authority of Campus Safety. Lead and coordinate campus emergency preparedness activities, including the development, maintenance, and periodic review of the University’s emergency response plan, evacuation and shelter-in-place procedures, fire drills, emergency exercises, and after-action reviews. Develop and deliver, or coordinate, safety and emergency preparedness training and awareness programs for students, faculty, staff, and Campus Safety personnel. Physical Security, Access, and Campus Operations Oversee the operational use and effectiveness of campus surveillance systems, access control measures, emergency communication platforms, visitor management processes, and other Campus Safety technologies. Identify vulnerabilities and recommend improvements in coordination with Operations, Facilities, Information Technology, and other appropriate departments. Oversee Campus Safety responsibilities related to campus access, Main Gate operations, building security, visitor management, and other physical security measures. Oversee enforcement of University parking and traffic regulations and support the safe and orderly movement of vehicles and pedestrians throughout campus. Coordinate Campus Safety staffing, security, traffic, and access needs for major University events, special events, outside activities, and other circumstances requiring enhanced safety measures. Compliance, Reporting, and Collaboration Monitor and support compliance with applicable federal, state, and local safety and security requirements, including University obligations under the Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act and related requirements. Responsibilities may include crime reporting and classification, required records and logs, Campus Security Authority processes, timely warnings, emergency notifications, and preparation of the Annual Security Report in collaboration with appropriate University departments. Conduct and/or coordinate regular security assessments and reviews to identify safety or security vulnerabilities and recommend appropriate improvements. Maintain appropriate departmental records and prepare reports regarding Campus Safety operations, staffing, incidents, trends, compliance activities, and improvement initiatives for University leadership. Maintain effective working relationships with local law enforcement agencies, fire and emergency medical services, and other public safety partners. Collaborate with departments throughout the University, including Student Engagement, Residence Life, Facilities, Human Resources, Title IX, Athletics, Campus Events, Information Technology, and others, regarding incidents, safety concerns, emergency preparedness, and campus operations. Perform other duties and responsibilities related to Campus Safety and University operations as assigned. Must abide by all University policies and procedures. Physical Requirements Ability to regularly move throughout campus buildings and grounds, including walking, standing, and navigating indoor and outdoor areas in varying weather conditions. Ability to respond to emergency situations and occasionally lift 50lbs or move security, emergency, or related equipment and materials. Minimum of 7-10 years of progressive experience in security management, law enforcement, or emergency management, with at least 3 years in a leadership role. Preferred: Bachelor's degree in Criminal Justice, Security Management, or a related field. Strong knowledge of campus safety regulations, emergency response procedures, and best practices in security operations. Proven ability to manage crisis situations calmly and effectively. Excellent leadership, communication, and interpersonal skills. Ability to collaborate with diverse groups and work effectively in a higher education environment. Proficiency in security technologies, including surveillance systems, access control, and incident reporting software. Relevant certifications (e.g., Certified Protection Professional (CPP), Certified Campus Security Professional (CCSP), etc.) are preferred. Valid driver’s license with a clean driving record. The allotted salary range for the position $85,000 to $95,000 Paid time off, including vacation and sick leave Medical, dental, and vision insurance Flexible Spending Account (FSA) Enhanced Short-Term Disability coverage Employer-paid Long-Term Disability coverage Voluntary Life Insurance Commuter benefits 403(b) Retirement Plan Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Professional development opportunities Tuition benefits for employees and their dependents"
    },
    "https://apply.workable.com/university-of-mount-saint-vincent/j/DD5BAD6BD7/": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Marketing | Tenure-track",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Marketing | Tenure-track. 503 - School of Business Founded by the Sisters of Charity of New York, the University of Mount Saint Vincent is an academically excellent, authentically inclusive, Catholic and ecumenical liberal arts university. The University combines a strong core curriculum with a full array of majors in the liberal arts and, within the tradition of liberal education, selected professional fields of study. At Mount Saint Vincent, a student’s education extends beyond knowledge, skills, and preparation for work. We seek the development of the whole person. In the spirit of Vincent de Paul and Elizabeth Ann Seton, we foster an understanding of our common humanity, a commitment to human dignity, and a full appreciation of our obligations to each other. Through its College of Professional and Continuing Studies, the University extends its primary undergraduate mission by offering high quality graduate studies in business, education and nursing and an array of undergraduate and certificate programs serving non-traditional students. Job Description: The School of Business invites applications for a 10 month, full-time, Marketing Faculty position. We seek an innovative educator and experienced marketing professional who can bring current industry practices, emerging technologies, and real-world business experiences into the classroom. The successful candidate will prepare students to think critically, communicate effectively, analyze data, make ethical decisions, and navigate an increasingly technology-driven marketplace. Responsibilities: Teach engaging undergraduate marketing courses in in-person and online formats. Develop innovative, student-centered, outcomes-based curricula. Incorporate experiential and project-based learning, including case studies, simulations, client projects, and other real-world applications. Provide meaningful and timely feedback on student work. Maintain regular in-person and virtual office hours and support student success. Stay current with emerging marketing practices, technologies, and industry trends. Collaborate with faculty colleagues on curriculum development and School of Business initiatives. Participate in professional development and contribute to the broader University community. Teaching 21 credits annually (fall/spring) in-person and online (synchronous/asynchronous) courses based on program needs. Candidates will be considered for appointment as Assistant Professor (Tenure-Track) or Instructor , based on their qualifications and experience. Assistant Professor (Tenure-Track): Terminal degree in Marketing or a related business field, with a master’s degree in Marketing or an MBA including at least 18 marketing credits; candidates completing a doctorate must defend by August 2027. Requires at least three years of college-level teaching and relevant marketing industry experience. Instructor: Graduate degree in Marketing or an MBA, with at least five years of college-level teaching and five years of marketing industry experience at the manager/director level or above. Strong commitment to excellence in teaching. Willingness to engage in university service. Ability to conduct scholarly research. Evidence of successful undergraduate teaching required. Professional experience in marketing or related business fields is an advantage. The annual salary range for this role is $70,000 - $85,000 Receive comprehensive medical, dental, and vision insurance Take advantage of a flexible Spending Account (FSA) Get peace of mind with Enhanced Short Term Disability Insurance Enjoy fully sponsored Life, AD&D and Long Term Disability insurance provided by the employer Voluntary Life Insurance coverage Save on commuting costs with Commuter Benefits Secure your financial future with employer contribution in a 403(b) Retirement Plan Get access to helpful support through the Employee Assistance Program"
    },
    "https://apply.workable.com/university-of-mount-saint-vincent/j/84D9762DA2/": {
      "title": "Visiting Assistant Professor of Accounting",
      "description": "Visiting Assistant Professor of Accounting. 503 - School of Business Founded by the Sisters of Charity of New York, the University of Mount Saint Vincent is an academically excellent, authentically inclusive, Catholic and ecumenical liberal arts University. The University combines a strong core curriculum with a full array of majors in the liberal arts and, within the tradition of liberal education, selected professional fields of study. At Mount Saint Vincent, a student’s education extends beyond knowledge, skills, and preparation for work. We seek the development of the whole person. In the spirit of Vincent de Paul and Elizabeth Ann Seton, we foster an understanding of our common humanity, a commitment to human dignity, and a full appreciation of our obligations to each other. Through its School of Professional and Continuing Studies, the University extends its primary undergraduate mission by offering high quality graduate studies in business, education and nursing and an array of undergraduate and certificate programs serving non-traditional students. Job Description The School of Business at the University of Mount Saint Vincent invites applications for a full-time Accounting faculty position. The anticipated start date is January 2027. The position will remain open until filled. We welcome applications from all areas of accounting, including but not limited to: Financial Accounting; Managerial and Cost Accounting; Auditing; Accounting Information Systems; Individual and Business Taxation; Forensic Accounting. Commensurate with qualifications and experience, applicants considered for the position of Visiting Professor in Accounting is a full-time academic year faculty position with a 21 credit teaching load, with the potential for tenure-track appointment. Applicants considered for the position of full-time Instructor will have a full-time 21 credit teaching load per academic year, non-tenurable position. Teaching load is usually a 4/3 or 3/4 Fall/Spring split. Responsibilities: Teach undergraduate and/or graduate accounting courses consistent with School of Business needs, faculty qualifications, and applicable accreditation and regulatory requirements; Demonstrate commitment to innovative, student-centered pedagogy, including experiential, project-based, and technology-enhanced learning approaches; Teach high-quality asynchronous online courses that foster engagement, instructor presence, and meaningful student interaction; Assess and grade assignments, projects, and exams with actionable feedback, returning them to students promptly; Provide in-person and virtual office hours each week to support students with guidance and refer them to support services as needed; Engage in ongoing professional development in both the academic discipline and teaching methods;Collaborate with other faculty members to align course requirements and contribute to departmental goals. A Master's degree in Business or Accounting is required, while a PhD or DBA is preferred Certified Public Accounting certification Experience teaching undergraduate accounting courses across various levels would also be advantageous The allotted salary range for for this position is $70,000 - $80,000 Medical, dental, vision insurance Flexible Spending Account (FSA) Enhanced Short Term Disability Insurance 100% employer sponsored Long Term Disability Voluntary Life Insurance Commuter Benefits Contribution in a 403(b) Retirement Plan Employee Assistance Program"
    },
    "https://apply.workable.com/university-of-mount-saint-vincent/j/0F69CC5F37/": {
      "title": "Coordinator for Alumnae/i Relations & Giving",
      "description": "Coordinator for Alumnae/i Relations & Giving. 605 - Alumnae Relations and Annual Giving Founded by the Sisters of Charity of New York, the University of Mount Saint Vincent is an academically excellent, authentically inclusive, Catholic and ecumenical liberal arts university. The University combines a strong core curriculum with a full array of majors in the liberal arts and, within the tradition of liberal education, selected professional fields of study. At Mount Saint Vincent, a student’s education extends beyond knowledge, skills, and preparation for work. We seek the development of the whole person. In the spirit of Vincent de Paul and Elizabeth Ann Seton, we foster an understanding of our common humanity, a commitment to human dignity, and a full appreciation of our obligations to each other. Position Summary: Reporting to the Senior Director for Alumnae/i Relations and Giving the Coordinator will contribute to the development of strategies designed to secure the engagement of alumnae/i and other friends in support of the University’s fundraising and strategic goals. The coordinator will be responsible for implementing those strategies by planning and executing special events and ongoing programs, developing persuasive communication materials, recruiting and cultivating volunteers, supervising student employees, and maintaining data necessary to monitor goals and achievements. This is a full-time, exempt position who is expected to work onsite, five days a week, Monday - Friday. Responsibilities: With the Senior Director and Assistant Director of Alumnae/i Relations and Giving, contribute to the development of integrated annual fund participation strategies and annual giving goals based upon analysis of past campaigns and constituency groups, in order to generate increasing levels and numbers of gifts from alumnae/i and friends. Work with the Alumnae/i Relations and Giving team to set a communication plan/schedule for the University’s annual giving initiatives. Ensure annual giving appeals are developed and deployed on time, and in alignment with that schedule. Draft and develop annual giving appeals and written materials necessary for annual giving solicitation. Collaborate with the Office of Communications and Marketing to ensure pieces align with the University’s editorial and brand guidelines. Maintain appeal, gift, and campaign details, as well as donor information in Raisers’ Edge database, ensuring proper documentation and tracking of annual fund operations; prepare reports tracking giving, progress towards goals, and pledge information. Maintain the schedule of annual giving reports for monitoring progress and completing necessary donor recognition. Implement, promote, and support alumnae/i events and programs that align with the University’s strategic plan and annual giving goals, including but not limited to on campus, local, and virtual programming as well as centerpiece programs such as Reunion and Homecoming Weekend, and affinity group events. Serve as lead at various alumnae/i events both on-campus and in the tri-state area. Assist in the scheduling, planning, and execution of a multi-year regional travel and event schedule. Together with the Senior Director and Assistant Director of Alumnae/i Relations and Giving, coordinate all University Athletic fundraising initiatives in collaboration with the Department of Athletics. Provide department leadership, coaches, and players assistance in the development and design of fundraising initiatives, and offer logistical/operational support to facilitate effective gift acknowledgment and record keeping. Support the engagement of the President’s Associates Council, a peer-to-peer fundraising initiative focused on cultivating, soliciting, and stewarding annual gifts of $1,000 or more. Plan, promote, and implement all phases of events (invitations, catering, scripts/programs, etc.) for the program. Oversee the student employment program including, hiring, training, supervising, and evaluating the Office Assistants and \"Dolphin Dialer\" phone team of approximately 8-14 students. Plan comprehensive training sessions for these students to serve as ambassadors of the University/Advancement team and for the Dolphin Dialers to secure contributions via telephone. Participate in identifying, recruiting, training and stewardship of alumnae/i class ambassadors and volunteers to engage graduates with the University in order to cultivate and solicit support of the Institution’s goals and objectives. Serve as back-up gift processor in the Office of Advancement, completing gift batches, financial deposits and acknowledgment production when necessary. Support the work of the Alumnae/i Association, its executive board and standing committees and chapters. Participate in University-wide and Advancement events, especially those related to fundraising. Successful candidates will have demonstrated relevant experiences related to developing engaging programming; drafting communications and appeals; supervising student workers; and recruiting and motivating volunteers. Strong oral and written communication skills, strong analytical skills, attention to detail and follow-up skills are required. Experience with Raisers Edge development software is desirable. Some evening and weekend work, and business travel is required. The successful candidate will demonstrate an understanding and genuine admiration of the mission of the University. Candidates should be creative, exercise discretion and good judgement, and be motivated to remain abreast of best practices in annual giving and current fundraising, direct marketing and volunteer management programs and practices used in the non-profit sector. Candidates should have an interest in a working environment which demands flexibility, encourages creativity and collegiality while emphasizing accountability for meeting goals. Bachelor’s degree required. Prior experience in higher education fundraising is preferred. Driver’s license required. Qualified graduates of the University are encouraged to apply. The annual salary for this position is $66,300. The University offers a comprehensive benefits package designed to support employees’ health, financial well-being, and work-life balance. Benefits for eligible employees include: Paid time off, including vacation and sick leave Medical, dental, and vision insurance Flexible Spending Account (FSA) Enhanced Short-Term Disability coverage Employer-paid Long-Term Disability coverage Voluntary Life Insurance Commuter benefits 403(b) Retirement Plan Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Professional development opportunities Tuition benefits for employees and their dependents Eligibility for benefits is subject to the terms and conditions of the applicable benefit plans and University policies."
    },
    "https://apply.workable.com/university-of-mount-saint-vincent/j/A54E8B2DC9/": {
      "title": "Pizza Cook",
      "description": "Pizza Cook. 710 - Food Service Founded by the Sisters of Charity of New York, the University of Mount Saint Vincent is an academically excellent, authentically inclusive, Catholic and ecumenical liberal arts university. The University combines a strong core curriculum with a full array of majors in the liberal arts and, within the tradition of liberal education, selected professional fields of study. At Mount Saint Vincent, a student’s education extends beyond knowledge, skills, and preparation for work. We seek the development of the whole person. In the spirit of Vincent de Paul and Elizabeth Ann Seton, we foster an understanding of our common humanity, a commitment to human dignity, and a full appreciation of our obligations to each other. Job Description: This position performs food service duties by preparing and serving meals for dining center operations and catered events using standardized recipes. Responsibilities include producing food in accordance with established service schedules while adhering to all health, safety, and sanitation regulations. The incumbent reviews menus and production schedules to determine the types and quantities of food required for daily operations. Additional responsibilities include preparing and processing food items, ensuring the proper use, handling, and storage of food products and supplies, and maintaining the cleanliness, organization, and sanitation of kitchen equipment, work areas, and food preparation spaces. Union - Local 100 Shift - 4pm - 12am Responsibilities: Food Preparation Prepare pizzas using standardized recipes and customer specifications. Prepare a variety of pizza doughs, sauces, and toppings. Wash, chop, slice, grill, and prepare vegetables, meats, and other ingredients. Prepare appetizers, salads, and other menu items as assigned. Monitor inventory levels and notify supervisors when supplies are low. Assist with menu development by recommending new recipes and specialty offerings, including vegan and gluten-free options. Food Safety & Sanitation Follow HACCP guidelines and all food safety and sanitation standards. Ensure proper food preparation, presentation, portion control, and serving temperatures. Properly handle, store, and rotate food products and supplies. Maintain clean, sanitized, and organized workstations, equipment, utensils, and serving areas. Clean spills and equipment promptly to maintain a safe work environment. Customer Service Provide courteous, professional, and efficient customer service. Serve food accurately, attractively, and in appropriate portions. Maintain knowledge of daily menu offerings and answer customer questions. Resolve customer concerns professionally and communicate feedback to supervisors. Maintain efficient service during peak meal periods while ensuring food displays remain clean and well-stocked. Standards & Operations Complete assigned duties accurately and on schedule. Follow all University policies, procedures, attendance, and safety requirements. Maintain a professional appearance and positive attitude. Assist with catered events and perform other duties as assigned. Ability to perform basic mathematical calculations, including addition, subtraction, multiplication, division, fractions, decimals, ratios, and percentages. Ability to read and understand written instructions, communicate effectively with customers and coworkers, and accurately complete basic written documentation. Physical Requirements Stand and walk for extended periods. Frequently use hands for food preparation and equipment operation. Bend, stoop, kneel, crouch, reach, and lift throughout the workday. Regularly lift up to 25 pounds; occasionally lift up to 50 pounds. Possess adequate vision, hearing, and communication abilities to safely perform job duties. Work Environment Work in a commercial kitchen with exposure to heat, humidity, noise, and moving equipment. Occasionally exposed to cleaning chemicals, fumes, temperature extremes, and other kitchen-related hazards. Follow all food safety, sanitation, and workplace safety procedures. Wear required personal protective equipment (PPE), including gloves, aprons, and eye protection, when appropriate. Safely operate kitchen equipment after receiving required training. Maintain a safe, clean, and hazard-free work environment for employees and customers. The allotted hourly range is $20 - $22 per hour Paid Time off (sick & vacation) Medical/Dental/Vision Pension plan"
    },
    "https://apply.workable.com/university-of-mount-saint-vincent/j/D41CFACA88/": {
      "title": "Director for Donor Engagement",
      "description": "Director for Donor Engagement. 601 - Institutional Advancement Founded by the Sisters of Charity of New York, the University of Mount Saint Vincent is an academically excellent, authentically inclusive, Catholic and ecumenical liberal arts university. The University combines a strong core curriculum with a full array of majors in the liberal arts and, within the tradition of liberal education, selected professional fields of study. At Mount Saint Vincent, a student’s education extends beyond knowledge, skills, and preparation for work. We seek the development of the whole person. In the spirit of Vincent de Paul and Elizabeth Ann Seton, we foster an understanding of our common humanity, a commitment to human dignity, and a full appreciation of our obligations to each other. Job Description: Reporting to the Senior Vice President for Advancement and External Relations, the Director for Donor Engagement is responsible for developing and implementing initiatives to identify, cultivate and solicit assigned prospects to ensure a strong base of ongoing financial support for institutional capital and major gift priorities. Responsibilities: Facilitate the University’s Major Gift Effort Work in collaboration with the Senior Vice President and President to create and implement cultivation, solicitation, and stewardship strategies necessary for a portfolio of assigned major gift prospects to grow philanthropic support for the University’s strategic priorities, as well as to identify new prospects for engagement. Develop proposals and solicit major gifts of $10,000 or more to support institutional priorities including capital campaigns, endowment, programmatic development and capital projects. Manage donor relationships to ensure appropriate stewardship activities are implemented to maintain and strengthen donor relationships with the University. Develop strategies and programs to educate current and prospective donors about planned giving opportunities and to increase the number of these commitments as members of the University’s Centennial Society. Work with University trustees, administration, faculty, and staff to translate University strategic priorities into development opportunities for major donors and donor prospects. In collaboration with the Senior Vice President and Associate Vice President for External Relations, coordinate with senior leadership at the University in support of fundraising initiatives and solicitations conducted outside the Office Advancement. Maintain and track activities in support of those collaborations within Raisers Edge, and documentation of solicitations, acknowledgements, and stewardship activities. Demonstrated success in soliciting, negotiating and closing major gifts and relationship-building skills with donor prospects; knowledge of planned giving vehicles and their benefits to donors; comprehensive campaign experience, and a record of successful collaboration with high level staff, trustees, officers, administrators, and key volunteers. A strong track record of developing and implementing donor-centered stewardship initiatives that increase gift levels and donor participation strongly preferred. Knowledge of best practices in fundraising, donor stewardship, and advancement strategy. Experience with CRM systems, preferably Banner, Raiser's Edge) and data-driven decision-making. Collaboration with Advancement office colleagues regarding proposal strategy and development, major donor components of alumni and fundraising events, and event planning as required. Candidate should be a creative, results oriented individual with strong interpersonal, written and oral communication skills; deadline and detail oriented; possess strong planning and organizational abilities and enjoy the challenge of setting and meeting goals. Must be flexible and adaptable, with the ability to work well within a team and independently; capable of representing the University in a variety of settings with confidence and diplomacy. Willingness to work evenings and weekends as needed for events and donor engagement. Ability to travel locally and nationally as required. Position requires the ability to exercise strong judgment, act in a principled, professional manner in working with sensitive, confidential information. A deep understanding of and commitment to the Catholic Liberal Arts mission of the University. Bachelor’s degree required; advanced degree preferred. Four to five years of direct fund-raising experience, preferably in non-profit or higher education setting. Salary Range: $115,000-$125,000 per year Benefits: Generous paid time off beyond the time the University is closed for holidays, including vacation, and sick leave. Comprehensive health, dental, and vision insurance. 100% employer sponsored Life Insurance & LTD benefits. 403(b) Retirement plan with employer matching. Professional development opportunities. Tuition benefits for employees and their dependents. Employee Assistance Program."
    },
    "https://apply.workable.com/university-of-mount-saint-vincent/j/817C65E7D7/": {
      "title": "Assistant Men Lacrosse Coach",
      "description": "Assistant Men Lacrosse Coach. 408 - Athletics and Recreation Founded by the Sisters of Charity of New York, the University of Mount Saint Vincent is an academically excellent, authentically inclusive, Catholic and ecumenical liberal arts college. The University combines a strong core curriculum with a full array of majors in the liberal arts and, within the tradition of liberal education, selected professional fields of study. At Mount Saint Vincent, a student’s education extends beyond knowledge, skills, and preparation for work. We seek the development of the whole person. In the spirit of Vincent de Paul and Elizabeth Ann Seton, we foster an understanding of our common humanity, a commitment to human dignity, and a full appreciation of our obligations to each other. Through its School of Professional and Continuing Studies, the University extends its primary undergraduate mission by offering high quality graduate studies in business, education and nursing and an array of undergraduate and certificate programs serving non-traditional students. The athletic department seeks an enthusiastic and committed Assistant Men’s Lacrosse Coach to join our NCAA Division III program. This part-time role is ideal for an individual passionate about lacrosse, player development, and contributing to a positive team culture. The Assistant Coach will work closely with the Head Coach to support all facets of the program, including practices, games, recruiting, and player mentorship, while maintaining the University’s commitment to academic and athletic excellence. This is a part-time, seasonal position running from September through May. Responsibilities: Assist the Head Coach in planning and running daily practices and skill development sessions. Support game-day operations, including setup, in-game coaching, and post-game breakdown. Aid in recruiting prospective student-athletes by attending select local events, assisting with on-campus visits, and communicating with recruits. Help foster a positive and competitive team environment, emphasizing sportsmanship, work ethic, and academic success. Comply with all NCAA, Skyline Conference, and University regulations. Perform other duties as assigned by the Head Coach. Previous playing or coaching experience in lacrosse (collegiate level preferred). Strong understanding of lacrosse fundamentals, strategy, and skill development. Ability to work collaboratively in a team-oriented environment. Excellent communication and organizational skills. Availability for practices, games, and occasional recruiting events, including evenings and weekends. The stipend for the position is $6,000 per season"
    },
